# Target C — audited §163 robust near-complete grid and zero-detour endpoint exclusion

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module normalizes the repaired theorem and hostile audit recorded in
`research/target_c_robust_nearcomplete_grid_zero_detour_endpoint_2026-09-12.md`
and its hostile-audit companion.

The reusable new statement is a stability version of the §76 complete-grid diameter
bound: a product grid may lose `o(D^2)` crossings and still obey the same leading
`2/sqrt(3)` shore-size limit. Applying that theorem to the equality structure in §154
excludes the formal zero-detour endpoint

\[
t_0={\pi\over2}+{2\over5}\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}
=1.9517214495404795\ldots .
\]

No explicit positive gap above `t_0` is claimed.

---

## 163.1 Robust near-complete-grid diameter theorem

Let `A_D,B_D` be two families of complete stopped-train centerlines in one source
configuration. Assume

\[
|A_D|=aD+o(D),
\qquad
|B_D|=bD+o(D),
\qquad a,b>0,
\tag{163.1}
\]

and, after choosing projective lifts,

\[
\alpha_u=\alpha_D+o(1)
\quad(u\in A_D),
\qquad
\beta_v=\alpha_D+{\pi\over3}+o(1)
\quad(v\in B_D).
\tag{163.2}
\]

Suppose all but `o(D^2)` pairs in `A_D x B_D` cross in actual strict unit rhombi.
Then

\[
\boxed{
a+b\le {2\over\sqrt3}.
}
\tag{163.3}
\]

### Phase separation with ties allowed

Put `L=pi/3`, `x_u=alpha_u`, and `y_v=beta_v-L`. Every actual cross-family strict
rhombus on this branch has

\[
y_v>x_u.
\]

Hence every inversion `x_u>=y_v` is a missing pair, and the number `M_D` of inversions
is `o(D^2)`.

For each occurrence `x` define

\[
L_D(x)=\#\{y:y\le x\}.
\]

If `M_D>0`, set

\[
\epsilon_D={M_D\over |A_D||B_D|},
\qquad
\delta_D=\sqrt{\epsilon_D}.
\]

Since `sum_x L_D(x)=M_D`, Markov deletes at most `delta_D|A_D|=o(D)` values with
`L_D(x)>delta_D|B_D|`. Let `x_*` be the largest retained value. Deleting the at most
`L_D(x_*)=o(D)` values `y<=x_*` leaves

\[
\boxed{
\max_{u\in A_D'}\alpha_u+L
<
\min_{v\in B_D'}\beta_v.
}
\tag{163.4}
\]

This argument is multiplicity-safe; it is the binding repair to the earlier informal
quantile formulation. Since both clusters shrink,

\[
\max_{u,v}(\beta_v-\alpha_u)<2L
\tag{163.5}
\]

for all large `D`.

### Minimum-degree pruning

After (163.4), let the remaining missing-pair count again be `M_D=o(D^2)`. Choose
`eta_D->0` with `M_D=o(eta_D D^2)` and delete every train missing more than
`eta_D D` opposite trains. Only `o(D)` trains are deleted. Thus, after relabeling,
there is `r_D=o(D)` such that

\[
\deg_A(u)\ge |B|-r_D,
\qquad
\deg_B(v)\ge |A|-r_D.
\tag{163.6}
\]

In particular every two and every three trains on one shore have a common neighbor on
the other shore.

### Global crossing orders from local complete subgrids

The strict angular inequalities (163.4)–(163.5) are exactly the §76 common-projection
hypotheses. Orient the `A` trains canonically and the `B` trains oppositely. One common
coordinate

\[
X(p)=e_D\cdot p
\]

strictly increases along every selected centerline, and all actual cross-family
crossings have one sign.

For two `A` trains, define their relative order by their crossings on any common `B`
neighbor. This is independent of the chosen common neighbor: two choices form an
actual signed `2 x 2` complete subgrid, so §76 gives the same order. A common neighbor
for every triple makes the relation transitive. Hence `A` has one global order. The
same argument gives one global order on `B`, and every actual neighbor list is the
restriction of the opposite global order.

### Opposite corner and projected path

Let `u_*` be the first `A` train, and let `v_*` be the last actual neighbor of `u_*`
in the global `B` order. Then their crossing is last among selected `B` crossings on
`u_*` and first among selected `A` crossings on `v_*`.

Follow `u_*` from its first selected `B` crossing to `u_* cap v_*`, then follow `v_*`
to its last selected `A` crossing. Every elementary centerline displacement on both
legs has positive `e_D` projection by the §76 cone estimate. Therefore omitted
cross-family pairs and any unselected intervening rhombi cannot decrease the projected
path length.

Exactly as in §76.5, the selected crossings contribute

\[
D\ge
\sum_{v\in N(u_*)}e_D\cdot v
+
\sum_{u\in N(v_*)}e_D\cdot u
-2.
\tag{163.7}
\]

The endpoints are centers of actual source rhombi, hence lie in the full source convex
hull of diameter `D`. Since the two direction clusters converge to axes separated by
`pi/3`,

\[
\min e_D\cdot u,
\quad
\min e_D\cdot v
\ge {\sqrt3\over2}-o(1).
\tag{163.8}
\]

Using (163.6) in (163.7) and dividing by `D` gives

\[
1\ge {\sqrt3\over2}(a+b),
\]

which proves (163.3).

---

## 163.2 Equality structure at the §154 zero-detour floor

Assume a Type-II survivor sequence has

\[
{b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to0
\tag{163.9}
\]

and, for contradiction,

\[
{q-k\over D}\to t_0.
\tag{163.10}
\]

Type II gives `per(F)/D->pi`, so `b/D->pi`. The exact outer ledger

\[
b+Q=2(q-k)
\]

therefore yields, writing

\[
s_2=\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}},
\]

\[
\boxed{
{Q\over D}\to {4\over5}s_2.
}
\tag{163.11}
\]

Audited §154 gives

\[
\liminf {Q+4f\over D}\ge4s_2.
\tag{163.12}
\]

Since `f<=Q`, also `Q+4f<=5Q`. Thus

\[
\boxed{
{Q+4f\over D}\to4s_2,
\qquad
{f\over D}\to{4\over5}s_2,
\qquad
Q-f=o(D).
}
\tag{163.13}
\]

The proof below uses the first equality, not a local pentagon obstruction.

Pass to a train-measure subsequence with

\[
T/D\to x
\]

and limiting projective train-direction measure having total atomic mass `sigma`.
Audited §142 gives

\[
x\sigma\ge2s_2,
\tag{163.14}
\]

while the §154 endpoint-capacity inequality and (163.13) give

\[
2x\sigma\le4s_2.
\tag{163.15}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{x\sigma=2s_2.}
\tag{163.16}
\]

Thus the absolute `D`-normalized atomic train mass is exactly `2s_2`.

---

## 163.3 Equality forces one phase orbit and a forbidden near-complete pair

Group atomic projective directions by the relation

\[
\phi\sim\phi+{\pi\over3}\pmod\pi.
\]

Let `A_j` be the total absolute atomic mass in orbit `j`, and let `E_j` be its
strict-rhombus crossing mass. The exact §142 relation support and triangle-free train
crossing graph give weighted Mantel

\[
E_j\le {A_j^2\over4}.
\tag{163.17}
\]

All strict-rhombus crossing mass is accounted for in these atomic relation pairs, so

\[
\sum_jE_j=s_2^2,
\qquad
\sum_jA_j=2s_2.
\]

Therefore

\[
s_2^2
\le {1\over4}\sum_jA_j^2
\le {1\over4}\left(\sum_jA_j\right)^2
=s_2^2.
\tag{163.18}
\]

Every inequality is equality. Hence exactly one `pi/3` orbit carries positive atomic
mass. It contains at most the three projective directions

\[
\phi_0,
\quad
\phi_0+{\pi\over3},
\quad
\phi_0+{2\pi\over3}
\pmod\pi,
\tag{163.19}
\]

with total absolute mass `2s_2`.

Choose shrinking disjoint atom neighborhoods in the standard fixed-window/diagonal
order. Their selected train graph has

\[
|W_D|=2s_2D+o(D),
\qquad
 e(W_D)={|W_D|^2\over4}-o(D^2).
\tag{163.20}
\]

Near-Mantel stability produces two shores of size `s_2D+o(D)` with only `o(D^2)`
missing cross pairs. A sufficiently small neighborhood of one atom has no internal
strict-rhombus crossing, so no atom can have linear mass on both Mantel shores.
With at most three atom groups and two shores, one shore consists of one atom of mass

\[
s_2D+o(D),
\]

while the other shore contains an atom of mass at least

\[
{s_2\over2}D+o(D).
\]

These two atom families differ by projective angle `pi/3` and have only `o(D^2)`
missing crossings. Applying §163.1 gives

\[
1\ge {\sqrt3\over2}\left(s_2+{s_2\over2}\right)
={3\sqrt3\over4}s_2.
\tag{163.21}
\]

But

\[
\left({3\sqrt3\over4}s_2\right)^2
={27\pi\over32\sqrt3}
>{81\over56}>1,
\]

using `pi>3` and `sqrt(3)<7/4`. Thus the right side of (163.21) is strictly greater
than one, a contradiction.

---

## 163.4 Endpoint exclusion

Therefore no unbounded audited-working Type-II survivor sequence can satisfy both

\[
{b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to0
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
{q-k\over D}
\to
{\pi\over2}+{2\over5}\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}.
}
\tag{163.22}
\]

The §154 zero-detour lower endpoint is unattainable. By the usual sequence/diagonal
argument this gives a qualitative separation from the formal endpoint inside the
zero-detour class, but §163 does not produce an explicit universal epsilon.

---

## Scope audit

- The phase-separation lemma is the repaired tie-safe Markov version.
- The robust grid theorem never assumes a complete product; completeness is used only
  on actual `2 x 2` subgrids to make local crossing order consistent.
- High minimum degree supplies common neighbors for every pair and triple.
- Missing selected crossings cannot reduce the path estimate because all intervening
  centerline displacements have positive common projection.
- The path endpoints are source-rhombus centers in the full source hull, so the diameter
  bound is the actual source diameter `D`.
- Equality in the orbitwise weighted-Mantel chain allows the three-atom split; the proof
  keeps it and uses only the larger atom on the two-atom shore.
- No local pentagon obstruction is used; the known near-three-axis pentagon regression
  remains valid.
- Contact-isolated source points are retained throughout and are not inserted into any
  core rigidity kernel.
- Positive normalized outer detour is not handled here.
- The Type-I inactive-container branch is not handled here.

**Disposition:** §163 PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. It gives a reusable
robust grid theorem and excludes the §154 zero-detour equality model, but C-II and
Level I remain open.
